# Build context for the runtime image.
#
# Two reasons to keep this tight, and both are load-bearing:
#
#  1. `node_modules/` holds native bindings (better-sqlite3, serialport)
#     compiled for the BUILD HOST. Letting them into the context means an
#     x86_64 workstation can silently ship its own .node files into an image
#     meant for a Raspberry Pi. The image installs and rebuilds its own
#     (see Dockerfile, stage `builder`); the host's must never leak in.
#  2. Context size is transfer time on every single build.
#
# The Dockerfile COPYs each path explicitly, so a new top-level directory is
# opt-in rather than opt-out — but keep this list honest anyway.

# --- never, under any circumstance ---
node_modules
**/node_modules
.git
.env
.env.*

# --- runtime state: lives in a volume, never in the image ---
data
logs
backups
dist

# --- machine-local runtime assets (gitignored, fetched by the postinstall) ---
# The builder stage fetches its own copy so the image is a function of the
# repo + the network, not of whatever happens to be lying around on the
# developer's disk. Leaving them in the context would also push a 30 MB
# soundfont through the daemon on every build — and, as observed during the
# R7 work, could ship a truncated stub straight into the image.
public/lib
assets/sf2/*.sf2
assets/sf2/*.partial
assets/sf2/*.download

# --- development / CI only, not needed to run the server ---
.github
.husky
coverage
tests
docs
wiki
images-a-faire
scripts/audit
.eslintrc.json
.prettierrc
.editorconfig
jest.config.cjs
vitest.config.js
vite.config.js
tsconfig.json
ecosystem.config.cjs
*.log

# --- the packaging files themselves ---
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore

# --- markdown at the root, except the notice the image must redistribute ---
# (`*.md` only matches the context root; assets/sf2/README.md, which carries
#  the soundfont's licence and provenance, is kept on purpose.)
*.md
!README.md
